---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout'
---

<BaseLayout
    title='What Is My IP Address?'
    description='Instantly see your public IP address (IPv4 and IPv6), learn how IP addresses work, why they change, and how to keep yours private.'
>
    <h1 class='text-center text-4xl md:text-6xl font-semibold my-8'>
        What Is My IP Address?
    </h1>

    <div class='grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-10'>
        <div class='rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-8 text-center'>
            <p class='text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400'>
                IPv4 Address
            </p>
            <p
                id='ipv4-value'
                class='animate-pulse mt-3 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold font-mono break-all'
            >
                Detecting…
            </p>
            <button
                type='button'
                data-copy='ipv4-value'
                class='mt-4 text-sm underline opacity-60 hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity'
            >
                Copy
            </button>
        </div>
        <div class='rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-8 text-center'>
            <p class='text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400'>
                IPv6 Address
            </p>
            <p
                id='ipv6-value'
                class='animate-pulse mt-3 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold font-mono break-all'
            >
                Detecting…
            </p>
            <button
                type='button'
                data-copy='ipv6-value'
                class='mt-4 text-sm underline opacity-60 hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity'
            >
                Copy
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class='text-center text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 -mt-4 mb-10'>
        Detected in your browser via the free ipify.org API. Your IP address is not stored by this site.
    </p>

    <article class='prose prose-lg md:prose-xl dark:prose-invert min-w-full py-4'>
        <h2>What is a public IP address?</h2>
        <p>
            Every device that talks to the internet needs an address. Your public IP address is the unique
            identifier your Internet Service Provider (ISP) assigns to your connection — it is what
            websites, apps, and online services see when you visit them. Think of it as a return address:
            when you request a page, the server knows where to send the data back.
        </p>
        <p>
            Do not confuse it with the private IP addresses (usually 192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x) that your
            router hands out to devices inside your home or office network. Private addresses work only
            within the local network. Your public IP is the one visible to the entire internet.
        </p>

        <h2>IPv4 vs IPv6</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>IPv4</strong> is the format most of the internet still uses: four groups of numbers, for
            example 203.0.113.42. It allows about 4.3 billion unique addresses — a pool that has run dry as
            the number of connected devices keeps growing.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>IPv6</strong> is its successor: much longer alphanumeric addresses, for example
            2001:db8::1. With 128 bits, it provides a virtually unlimited number of addresses and was
            created specifically to solve the shortage. Many connections today use both, which is why this
            page shows two results — if IPv6 says "Not detected", your network is IPv4-only.
        </p>

        <h2>Is this address permanent?</h2>
        <p>
            Almost all home users have a dynamic IP address: the ISP can change it periodically, for example
            when your router restarts or when the lease time expires. Businesses and servers, on the other
            hand, often pay for a static IP that never changes — useful for hosting a website, running
            remote access to an office network, or keeping services reachable at the same address.
        </p>

        <h2>Keep it private</h2>
        <p>
            Your IP address does not reveal your home address, but it does expose your approximate location
            (usually city level) and your ISP. Sharing it publicly can invite unwanted attention, from
            port scanning to denial-of-service attacks. A practical way to hide it is a VPN — and the best
            part is that the technology behind most trustworthy VPNs, such as WireGuard or OpenVPN, is
            open source. Share your IP only where it is really needed.
        </p>

        <h2>Why check your public IP?</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> confirm whether your ISP has assigned you a new address or
                your connection behaves as expected.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Router and firewall setup:</strong> port forwarding, firewall rules, and parental
                controls often require knowing your current public IP.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Remote access and VPNs:</strong> connect to your home network from anywhere, or open
                this page while your VPN is on and off to verify it actually masks your address.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Security monitoring:</strong> track changes to a dynamic IP to spot unexpected
                activity on your connection.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
    const fetchIp = async (url: string): Promise<string> => {
        try {
            const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(6000) })
            if (!res.ok) return ''
            const data = await res.json()
            return data.ip || ''
        } catch {
            return ''
        }
    }

    const setValue = (id: string, value: string) => {
        const el = document.getElementById(id)
        if (!el) return
        el.classList.remove('animate-pulse')
        el.textContent = value || 'Not detected'
    }

    const detect = async () => {
        const [v4, v6] = await Promise.all([
            fetchIp('https://api.ipify.org?format=json'),
            fetchIp('https://api6.ipify.org?format=json')
        ])
        setValue('ipv4-value', v4)
        setValue('ipv6-value', v6)
    }

    detect()

    document.querySelectorAll('[data-copy]').forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
            const targetId = btn.getAttribute('data-copy')
            const target = targetId ? document.getElementById(targetId) : null
            const text = target?.textContent?.trim()
            if (!text || text === 'Detecting…' || text === 'Not detected') return
            try {
                await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)
                btn.textContent = 'Copied!'
                setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'Copy'), 1500)
            } catch {
                /* clipboard unavailable */
            }
        })
    })
</script>
